{"answer":"The watch that sits on the dock every morning is in charge of computing the 10% limit. Every fisher, myself included, turns up at dawn, and we take the figures from last night’s haul, estimate how many fish are left in the lake, and calculate ten percent of that total. The result is written on the community board at the dock and read aloud to every fisher before any boat sets out. That way everyone knows the hard cap for the day.



I do not change the proposal.



", "revised_policy":"Each fisher may only take up to 10% of the lake's current total fish stock per trip, and the community may not exceed 20% of the stock in a single day. Before each trip fishers report intended catch and the community calculates 10% of the current lake total. After the trip, the actual catch is recorded. If a fisher exceeds their 10% limit, they must return the excess to the lake. The community monitors the total daily catch; if it goes beyond 20%, the group meets to redistribute remaining quota and imposes a temporary catch reduction for non‑compliant fishers. Compliance is enforced by a rotating community watch who checks logs and observes fishing. Violations incur a small penalty of 5% of next month’s earnings.", "revised_operationalization":"Before each trip, the rotating watch meets at dawn to calculate the 10% limit from last night’s catch. The limit is posted on the community board and announced. Each fisher writes down his intended catch. After fishing, fishers log their actual haul. If a fisher exceeds his limit, the excess is returned immediately. Daily totals are tallied; if the 20% threshold is breached, the community convenes to reallocate remaining quota and impose temporary reductions for those who over‑caught.", "reasoning":"I answered as a community member who follows the established process. The proposal remains unchanged because the question only asked who calculates and how it is communicated, not the policy itself."}

{"answer":"We decide ban length by how serious the infraction was. First offense gets a warning. Second offense triggers a one‑day fishing ban. Third offense a three‑day ban. A fourth infraction means a permanent ban unless the fisher petitions and the community votes to lift it. The banned fisher may resume fishing only after the ban period has passed, after the community confirms the reserve net still holds 200 kg and that the logbook shows no further discrepancies, and after the Reserve Keeper records a compliance check. If all those checks are clear, the community votes to lift the ban and the fisher can return to the lake.

This keeps penalties predictable, lets us enforce rules fairly, and ensures we’re all watching the reserve and logs the same way.

", "revised_policy":"Each fisher may take no more than 15 kg per trip and the lake must keep at least 200 kg in reserve at all times. Maintain a shared logbook where each fisher records catch weight and time; weigh fish at the dock; community checks daily that total fish removed plus the reserve net equals the estimated lake stock. Reserve net holds 200 kg of fish visibly marked; a designated Reserve Keeper—rotated weekly by community vote—is responsible for physically maintaining the reserve net and weighing it each morning; peer enforcement with warnings and temporary fishing bans for non‑compliance. Reserve Keeper will weigh the 200 kg reserve net each morning, record the weight in the shared logbook, and ensure the net is properly stocked and marked. The community will inspect the reserve net together every morning before fishing starts, confirming it holds the required 200 kg and cross‑checking with the logbook entries. All fishers are expected to follow the 15 kg trip limit and report any discrepancies immediately.", "revised_operationalization":"As above. The Reserve Keeper weighs the net each morning, records it in the logbook, and verifies the net is stocked and marked. The community inspects the net every morning, confirms 200 kg, and cross‑checks the logbook. Fisher logs catch weight/time. Non‑compliance triggers warning, then temporary bans as outlined. Banned fisher may resume only after ban period, community review, and verified compliance.", "reasoning":"I think a clear escalation keeps us honest and fair, and the community review after the ban ensures we’re all on the same page about the reserve and logs. This way we protect the lake and each other."}

{"answer":"The lake stock is estimated each morning by the Reserve Keeper, who takes the weight of the reserve net and then adds up the catch weights recorded by every fisher in the shared logbook for the previous day. He subtracts the reserve net weight from the total fish removed to get the net amount taken from the lake. Adding this to the stock estimate from the prior day gives the new stock estimate, which he writes into the logbook for the community to see. If the estimate shows the reserve net is below 200 kg or the total removed exceeds the allowed 15 kg per fisher, the Keeper flags a discrepancy for correction.

In short: Reserve Keeper → weigh reserve net → sum logbook catches → compute new stock estimate → record in logbook.
","revised_policy":"Each fisher may take no more than 15 kg per trip and the lake must keep at least 200 kg in reserve at all times. Maintain a shared logbook where each fisher records catch weight and time; weigh fish at the dock; community checks daily that total fish removed plus the reserve net equals the estimated lake stock. Reserve net holds 200 kg of fish visibly marked; a designated Reserve Keeper—rotated weekly by community vote—is responsible for physically maintaining the reserve net and weighing it each morning; peer enforcement with warnings and temporary fishing bans for non‑compliance. Reserve Keeper will weigh the 200 kg reserve net each morning, record the weight in the shared logbook, and ensure the net is properly stocked and marked. The community will inspect the reserve net together every morning before fishing starts, confirming it holds the required 200 kg and cross‑checking with the logbook entries. All fishers are expected to follow the 15 kg trip limit and report any discrepancies immediately. Non‑compliance triggers warning, then temporary bans as outlined. Banned fisher may resume only after ban period, community review, and verified compliance. The Reserve Keeper, in consultation with the community, will issue warnings and enforce temporary bans, and oversee the review process for resumption of banned fishers.","revised_operationalization":"Reserve Keeper records the daily lake stock estimate in the shared logbook after calculating it by summing all fishers' reported catches for the day and adding the reserve net weight, then subtracting the reserve net weight to find the net fish removed. The Reserve Keeper adds this to the previous day's estimate to generate the current day's stock estimate, writes it into the logbook, and alerts the community if the estimate falls short of the reserve requirement or exceeds the allowable removal limits.","reasoning":"The question asks who estimates the lake stock and how it's generated and recorded. In our established system the Reserve Keeper has the role of weighing the reserve net, aggregating catch data from the logbook, and computing the daily stock estimate. Recording this estimate in the logbook provides transparency and allows the community to verify compliance. I kept the original policy unchanged because the answer does not alter any rules."}

